The Android host test downloaded JUnit with no verification.

CI run on 132c145, job android-sdk:

  WARN: Error while reading zip file: /tmp/junit-4.13.2.jar:
        java.lang.IndexOutOfBoundsException
  WARN: Error while reading zip file: /tmp/hamcrest-core-1.3.jar: ...
  BehaviorVectorsTest.kt:4:12: error: unresolved reference 'junit'

The jars arrived corrupt. run_host_test.sh fetched them with "curl -sL", which
writes a file whether or not it got the jar, and guarded the fetch with
"[ -f ]", which accepts a truncated one. kotlinc then reported "unresolved
reference" against a test file that was CORRECT. A supply failure was reported
as a source failure, and the newest test file took the blame purely for being
the first to import junit.

Fixed by pinning both digests and verifying after download. The values were
cross-checked against Maven Central's own published .sha1 rather than trusting
a single local download:

  junit-4.13.2.jar      sha256 8e495b634469d64fb8acfa3495a065cbacc8a0fff55ce1e31007be4c16dc57d3
  hamcrest-core-1.3.jar sha256 66fdef91e9739348df7a096aa384a5685f4e875584cce89386a7a47251c4d8e9
  upstream .sha1 8ac9e16d933b6fb43bc7f576336b8f4d7eb5ba12 == locally computed sha1

Controls, measured as real exit codes rather than through a pipeline, because
reading an exit code through "| head" reports head's status and not the
script's, which is a mistake made twice in this same session:

  expected digest deliberately wrong -> exit 1, and the message names the supply
     failure and says explicitly that it is not a compile error in the sources
  restored                           -> exit 0, OK (79 tests)
  a truncated cached jar             -> detected and re-downloaded, then the
     suite passed, so the cache repairs itself rather than failing

This also closes an integrity hole: an unverified curl into a world-writable
/tmp path let the build compile against a substituted jar.

AFFECTED-SIDE PROOF. The corruption was a CI-side transient: the jars
download fine on this machine, so run_host_test.sh exits 0 at the parent
and cannot itself be the affected-side test. The proof is structural, and
asserts both that every jar URL is digest verified AND that a pinned digest
is actually compared rather than merely present, because a constant nobody
checks is the same shape as the guard that matched its own comment.

  parent version (unverified curl) -> FAILED (failures=1)
  this version                     -> OK (6 cases)
